00:30:20,570 --> 00:30:22,970
Accordingly, we need a screwdriver or drill.

410
00:30:24,770 --> 00:30:27,470
Because we need to drill holes for fastening bolts.

411
00:30:29,930 --> 00:30:32,470
Fastening the board itself and the transistors.

412
00:30:34,090 --> 00:30:36,550
And I attach the step-up transformer with a bolt.

413
00:30:38,810 --> 00:30:39,970
That's the first thing.

414
00:30:41,210 --> 00:30:42,650
What drills will we need?

415
00:30:44,250 --> 00:30:46,770
We'll need a 3.2mm drill.

416
00:30:49,130 --> 00:30:53,070
So that these bolts with 3mm outer size pass through.

417
00:30:57,810 --> 00:31:00,450
And we'll also need a 2.6mm drill.

418
00:31:11,650 --> 00:31:14,170
You can buy 2.5mm in stores.

419
00:31:16,190 --> 00:31:18,330
So we drill the hole in the heat sink.

420
00:31:21,600 --> 00:31:26,020
And we could, with the tap, the M3 thread, cut a thread there.

421
00:31:27,920 --> 00:31:29,340
Tap the thread carefully.

422
00:31:29,860 --> 00:31:31,320
If it's aluminum, the metal will not cut through.

423
00:31:31,320 --> 00:31:34,040
Aluminum metal is viscous and you can easily break the tap.

424
00:31:34,580 --> 00:31:37,420
So I'd suggest greasing it with some lubricant when tapping.

425
00:31:39,060 --> 00:31:41,380
Simplest is to use WD-40.

426
00:31:44,980 --> 00:31:47,520
Two drills and the tap, don't forget.

427
00:31:48,180 --> 00:31:51,180
Very helpful for soldering small parts.

428
00:31:56,720 --> 00:32:00,040
Cutting tools, side cutters and small round nose pliers.

429
00:32:02,660 --> 00:32:06,460
The wire I used for the 5.5 turn primary winding.

430
00:32:09,550 --> 00:32:12,410
Single core, its core is 1.1mm.

431
00:32:19,640 --> 00:32:22,840
And it's 1.8mm with insulation outside.

432
00:32:25,160 --> 00:32:28,980
The wire I wound the step-up secondary with, this red one.

433
00:32:32,060 --> 00:32:33,520
Now let's measure it too.

434
00:32:37,660 --> 00:32:39,120
0.7mm core, 1.8mm.

435
00:32:39,140 --> 00:32:41,700
And 1.4mm with insulation.

436
00:32:52,820 --> 00:32:55,460
When wound on this core, here's how it's laid.

437
00:32:58,820 --> 00:33:02,960
First layer turn to turn, with no gaps around the full circumference.

438
00:33:03,740 --> 00:33:07,260
Then the second layer also from the inside, turn to turn.

439
00:33:07,780 --> 00:33:10,720
And the third layer is laid with this wire turn to turn.

440
00:33:11,140 --> 00:33:12,800
So it's wound in three layers.

441
00:33:13,520 --> 00:33:17,860
And that was enough on this core for us to get 220V on the lamp under load.

442
00:33:20,990 --> 00:33:24,450
You adjust the voltage individually with the turn count.

443
00:33:25,370 --> 00:33:27,590
If the wire is slightly different, no problem.

444
00:33:28,950 --> 00:33:35,170
Besides buying the radio parts per the list of this schematic, don't forget to mandatorily get KPT thermal paste.

445
00:33:40,110 --> 00:33:43,830
Mine is KPT-19 non-drying thermal paste.

446
00:33:44,850 --> 00:33:49,590
And if you can find mica insulating pads for transistors, though they're rare now.

447
00:33:51,270 --> 00:33:55,170
Most often you can find this material that has polyethylene on top.

448
00:33:56,250 --> 00:33:57,870
It protects from dust.

449
00:33:59,510 --> 00:34:02,750
This is a thermal conductive material while being insulating.

450
00:34:04,470 --> 00:34:07,910
Because you can't mount transistors directly on the heat sink.

451
00:34:08,610 --> 00:34:13,050
Because this lead, where the bolt is fastened, is connected to the current.

452
00:34:16,390 --> 00:34:19,950
And if you fasten directly to metal, there will be a short circuit.

453
00:34:19,950 --> 00:34:22,150
So a gasket is mandatory.

454
00:34:23,390 --> 00:34:28,310
The gasket is placed at least 3mm beyond this contact, the cooling surface.

455
00:34:32,950 --> 00:34:37,410
Smeared with thermal paste, and the transistor is installed on this substrate.

456
00:34:38,590 --> 00:34:40,430
And there's another small thing you might forget.

457
00:34:42,910 --> 00:34:45,030
These plastic insulator washers.

458
00:34:47,970 --> 00:34:51,610
They go on the screw that fastens the transistor to the heat sink.

459
00:34:55,780 --> 00:34:59,420
And when in place, the gasket sits in the transistor's eye.

460
00:35:02,700 --> 00:35:06,860
And prevents contact between the fastening bolt and the metal lead of the transistor.

461
00:35:12,250 --> 00:35:16,730
Also a critical thing, because if you just screw it in, there'll be a short circuit.

462
00:35:19,350 --> 00:35:20,910
